# Prove localhost security and privacy

## Scope and existing evidence

Test loopback binding, session token, hostile local pages, file-path
confinement, temporary cleanup, no telemetry, no autosave, and no undeclared
network access.

Current loopback-runtime evidence requires the exact canonical Host, browser
Origin, and Bearer credential before protected draft mutation. The request line
requires exactly one ASCII space between method, target, and HTTP/1.1; full
0–255 byte sweeps now pin both separators to that one byte and reject every byte
after the HTTP version. An independent 2,048-case byte-substitution matrix pins
all eight `HTTP/1.1` bytes exactly.

GET and POST are case-sensitive across every ASCII case variant. A separate
1,792-case byte-substitution matrix pins every byte of both admitted method
names. Tabs, repeated spaces, trailing whitespace, and other methods or versions
reject before routing.

The loopback HTTP CSP previously sent only frame and connection restrictions;
its stricter script, style, form, object, worker, and resource rules existed
solely in an HTML meta element. A red-first Rust regression now compares the
served HTML policy to the HTTP policy, allowing the header-only
`frame-ancestors 'none'` exception. The transport now enforces all resource
restrictions in the HTTP header on success, error, and empty responses, even
without interpreting the HTML meta tag. This does not remedy the separate
browser-opener argv credential exposure.

A real Firefox header-only CSP fixture now confirms that the actual runtime
response blocks foreign JavaScript, images, and stylesheets without an HTML
meta tag. A permissive control tab successfully fetches those same three
resources, while the protected tab emits zero additional requests. The same
header-only tab also rejects an inserted inline script and a hostile `<base>`
URL; a permissive tab executes the script and changes its base URL. A
same-origin JavaScript worker also runs in the permissive tab but is refused
without any worker request in the header-only tab, independently exercising
`worker-src 'none'`.

A same-origin child frame and form POST likewise succeed
in the permissive control and fail on the header-only tab without generating
requests. Both checks fail independently when the corresponding directive is
relaxed to `'self'`, pinning `frame-src 'none'` and `form-action 'none'` rather
than incidental cross-origin restrictions.

The real Firefox fixture also loads valid same-origin PCM WAV metadata in the
permissive tab. The header-only tab denies that media without a resource
request, and deliberately relaxing `media-src 'none'` to `'self'` makes the
regression fail. This isolates HTTP media enforcement without testing audio
playback or claiming to secure the platform browser opener.

A same-origin WOFF2 resource is also fetched by the permissive tab through
`FontFace.load()` despite an intentionally invalid font payload. The protected
tab makes no font request; relaxing `font-src 'none'` to `'self'` causes an
additional request and fails the regression. This proves font fetch refusal,
not custom-font decoding or rendering.

The same header-only Firefox fixture also probes a same-origin embedded SVG
`<object>`. The permissive control loads the SVG document; the protected page
neither loads it nor requests the resource. Changing only `object-src 'none'` to
`'self'` makes the regression fail, independently pinning the HTTP directive
rather than the default same-origin resource policy.

The Firefox fixture also copies the runtime response's
`X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` value onto a synthetic same-origin script
served with `text/plain`. A control without the header executes that script;
the resource with the runtime header is fetched but not executed. Removing
only `nosniff` makes the assertion fail. This checks independent browser MIME
enforcement using a rehosted resource, not a claim that Atrament serves
misdeclared scripts in production.

This is browser enforcement evidence for the HTTP boundary, not a claim about
browser-opener credentials or arbitrary outbound desktop programs.

Browser privacy regression evidence also pins the generated workspace to exactly
four admitted same-origin `fetch` call sites: handshake, draft read,
draft write, and explicit session end. All four omit ambient credentials,
reject redirects, and use no-store behavior. The three POSTs use origin-only
referrers, while the read-only draft GET uses no-referrer. The browser
source and generated modules have no absolute HTTP(S), beacon, XHR,
WebSocket, or EventSource transport path.

A follow-up regression inventories all five authored TypeScript modules and
their five emitted JavaScript counterparts. New source or emitted modules
require review, and only the main module may retain four admitted fetch sites
and their security metadata. Inert network and persistent-storage additions to
a helper separately fail the guard in either representation, including before
a TypeScript rebuild. These are source-level checks, not continuous outbound
network observation.

Malformed or misdeclared browser handshake responses cannot grant edit
authority. The in-memory client now offers explicit same-tab retry on rejected
`200`/`409` JSON metadata while preserving the fragment-free address bar;
Firefox confirms wrong-media and oversized responses never request private
drafts before a later valid handshake. This is client recovery evidence, not a
change to the session's HTTP authentication rules.

A real Firefox HTTP `409` fixture now distinguishes a malformed
incompatibility body, which permits an explicit same-tab retry, from a
complete typed version-mismatch diagnostic, which is terminal. Both stages
keep the launch fragment removed and all three draft fields blank and
disabled; even a synthetic click on the hidden terminal retry control sends
no further handshake or private draft request. This proves the browser
state boundary, not independent backend version interoperability.

A discarded-response browser regression now verifies that wrong-media
handshakes, draft responses, and unexpected statuses are refused without
reading their bodies, and that the browser explicitly cancels their
streams. The authenticated handshake and draft requests also
compose page-lifetime and per-request abort signals; refusing an already-started
fetch aborts the request instead of depending on `ReadableStream.cancel()` to
close its connection.

In a real Firefox fixture, a server deliberately sends headers and one partial
wrong-media handshake body, then never finishes it.
The browser refuses editing, aborts the live connection, and retries in the same
fragment-free tab before hydrating its drafts. The synthetic server sees no
private draft request before the valid retry. This narrows the browser network
lifecycle evidence; it does not remove the independently observed opener-argv
credential exposure or prove packet-level isolation across all future paths.

A source-level backend network-surface guard now requires every production
`std::net` import to remain inside the loopback session runtime. Locked offline
Cargo metadata pins each direct registry dependency's owner, exact version
requirement, source, feature/default-feature, optional, target, kind, and rename
metadata, plus the complete resolved registry package name/version/source set.
Direct or transitive dependency changes therefore
require explicit privacy-surface review. This does not replace packet-level
offline execution evidence.

The backend source guard also confines standard-library process authority to
the single browser-launch adapter. A synthetic `std::process::Command` in the
session-secret adapter passed the earlier network/dependency suite, but the new
process inventory rejects it; restoring the source returns the suite to green.
Equivalent direct, grouped and aliased process imports are covered separately.
This is a conservative source-level spawn guard, not a guarantee against every
native or indirect process API, nor a fix for the known browser-opener
argv leak.

A further source-level guard refuses renaming the `std` crate root anywhere in
backend Rust, including direct `use std as ...`, `extern crate std as ...`, and
grouped `use std::{self as ...}` imports. Such aliases could hide network,
process, or filesystem authority from the reviewed detectors without adding a
new dependency. A red-first tracked-source fixture in `session-secret` using a
renamed `std::net` type was rejected, then fully restored; nested unrelated
`self as` imports remain permitted. This is static inventory evidence, not a
Rust parser, runtime syscall boundary, or solution for the opener-argv leak.

A follow-up red-first source regression detects Unix `FileExt` positional
writes and `nix::unistd` write/truncate functions imported directly, in
groups, or behind a module alias. The actual Rust compiler accepts stable
`write_at` and `write_all_at` methods, and five transient backend source
variants each trigger the confined file-mutation allowlist before restoration.
Normal positional reads and `nix::unistd::read` remain admitted. This is a
review gate, not a full dataflow proof, syscall sandbox, or fix for opener
argument visibility.

The pinned `nix` crate also exposes metadata, ownership, link, directory, and
rename effects through `sys::stat`, `unistd`, and `fcntl`. Red-first source
checks now catch those named calls and disguised imports, including grouped
aliases of a whole module. Six temporary `session-secret` variants were
rejected before exact restoration; read-only imports remain admitted. The
result only narrows declared Rust source authority, not ambient file access
or the separately documented browser-opener argument leak.

An additional red-first source gate inventories generic `std::io::Write`
imports and stream-writing methods, including raw names and grouped imports.
It keeps the browser bootstrap, loopback HTTP transport, and explicit profile
file-commit adapter as the only reviewed owners. Six transient writes or
aliases in `session-secret` fail before byte-identical restoration, while
`std::fmt::Write` and read-only I/O imports remain permitted. This is not a
file-descriptor dataflow analysis or a fix for the opener-argument leak.

A separate red-first native-source regression detects renaming or importing
the entire `nix::sys` namespace, including nested `self as` and grouped
imports. That alias could hide `stat` mutation or future socket APIs from
nix-qualified checks. Six injected `session-secret` variants each fail before
exact byte restoration; specific read-only stat imports are still allowed.
This adds a source-review boundary, not a Rust name resolver, syscall sandbox,
or protection for opener command-line credentials.

Globbed authority imports now also require review: `std::*`,
`std::fs::*`, `std::io::*`, and the corresponding locked `nix` modules may
expose filesystem or native operations without naming the individual API in
the importing file. A compile-only Rust probe confirmed root and nested
glob syntax, and six temporary `session-secret` additions fail the new
backend inventory before exact restoration. Read-only explicit imports and
unrelated formatting globs remain allowed. This is static declaration review,
not runtime syscall confinement or a remedy for the known opener-argv leak.

The inventory now also detects grouped absolute-root `use ::std::{...}`
network and process imports, plus recognized Unix-domain socket creation
and `std::os::unix::net` imports. Initially, all three synthetic import forms
escaped the respective checks; a red-first fixture then rejected each form
in the temporary `session-secret` source before restoring its exact bytes.
Nested Unix socket imports and non-ASCII `std` root aliases have independent
positive checks; ordinary Unix filesystem and descriptor metadata imports
remain permitted. This closes known source-scan gaps, not arbitrary local IPC,
FFI use, or the unrelated opener-argv credential exposure.

Rust also permits `r#` spellings of ordinary library module and crate
identifiers, including `r#std`, `r#net`, and `r#process`; the pinned compiler
accepts these spellings. Red-first source regressions now canonicalize the
raw-identifier form before reviewing networking, process creation, Unix
sockets, standard-library root aliases, filesystem mutation, and native FFI.

Six transient `session-secret` imports using these bypass spellings each fail
the backend allowlist, and the tracked source is restored byte-for-byte.
This extends conservative source review; it is not a Rust parser or a syscall
sandbox and does not settle launcher-argument credential exposure.

A further backend privacy inventory now flags unreviewed native authority:
`libc` and `nix::libc` raw syscall access (including aliased imports),
`nix::sys::socket` references, renamed `nix` roots, C/system extern blocks,
and direct inline assembly. The pinned `nix` dependency exposes libc even
without new socket Cargo features, so a new FFI path can bypass both std-only
source guards and locked dependency inventory.

A test-owned inactive
`nix::libc::socket` import in `session-secret` passed the prior guard; the
new guard rejects it, a native extern declaration, and a grouped `nix::libc`
alias, and removal restores the original source bytes. Ordinary reviewed
`nix::fcntl` and `nix::sys::stat` imports remain permitted. This is a
conservative source scanner, not a Rust AST analysis, syscall sandbox,
packet-level isolation proof, or a fix for opener-argv exposure.

Rust source-inventory completeness now also rejects unreviewed `include!`
macro expansion and `#[path]`/`#[cfg_attr]` source redirection in any backend
`.rs` file, without prohibiting the reviewed static `include_bytes!` assets.
Cargo metadata admission rejects new workspace custom build-script targets,
which could run code outside the inspected runtime sources. Red-first source
injections and a test-only explicit Cargo build target all passed the old
inventory and fail the new guards; the original Rust source and Cargo manifest
were restored byte-for-byte. These are conservative declaration-level checks,
not an exhaustive compiler-expansion audit or a build sandbox.

A Linux process-composition fixture now admits a deterministic successful
platform opener, waits for the live Atrament process to publish `ready`, and
resolves only its `/proc/<pid>/fd` socket inodes through that process's kernel
TCP/UDP tables. The ready process owns exactly one Internet socket: the
published IPv4 `127.0.0.1` TCP listener, with no connected, UDP, IPv6, wildcard,
or second Internet socket descriptor. This is live process evidence, not a
packet-level proof that every future execution path is offline.

A separate Linux lifecycle regression captures the synthetic opener's fresh
credential only through a test-owned Unix-domain socket, without a persistent
credential file or logged URL. It then performs an authenticated compatible
handshake, a private task replacement, a private task readback, and explicit
session termination against the real compiled runtime.

At startup and after
each completed stateful request, the runtime process owns exactly one IPv4
loopback listener plus at most one inbound loopback connection, with no
outbound, non-loopback, IPv6, or UDP Internet socket descriptor. An
intentionally incomplete public request stays open with an accepted inbound
socket alongside that listener, then completes successfully. Every
socket descriptor beyond the inherited standard streams must appear in the
matching TCP, UDP, or raw kernel tables. The harness's standard output and
error streams use Unix socketpairs, so descriptors 0-2 are excluded explicitly;
future unclassified product-owned socket descriptors still fail the fixture.

The process exits after authenticated End. These stage-wise socket snapshots
are not continuous packet observation. They also do not prove the production
browser opener protects its credential from process-argument inspection.

A checked-in scripted Firefox fixture without a session credential now observes
exactly seven same-origin resource requests: the document, stylesheet, main
module, and four generated support modules. It emits no session API or hostile
text request, so the active shell's resource graph is executable evidence rather
than source inspection alone.

Malformed bare-LF or bare-CR header line endings reject while the peer remains
connected instead of occupying the listener until the request timeout. EOF also
cannot replace the required blank CRLF header terminator. Exhaustive prefix and
two-write split fixtures cover every byte boundary of valid GET and body-bearing
POST requests.

A socket-level incomplete-body fixture now pins transport error classification:
an early write-side EOF returns `400`, while the same incomplete body held open
past its read budget returns `408`. Both responses retain the common no-store,
frame, referrer, nosniff, and connection-close security headers.

Configured request and response timeouts are total transport deadlines rather
than per-read or per-write budgets. Trickle bytes cannot extend the production
two-second request budget, and a slow response consumer cannot hold the
single-thread listener open by making intermittent write progress. The reader
and writer both refuse to run without their configured deadline; accepted
connections receive both deadlines before request handling begins. Five
production trials with a one-mebibyte authenticated draft and tiny-window
trickle readers released queued health in 1.932-1.980 seconds instead of
remaining blocked beyond eight seconds.

The transport admits at most 16 KiB of request headers and 2 MiB of request
body. Exact-limit fixtures pass while one byte over either ceiling rejects.

All current `GET` routes are explicitly bodyless across public, private-draft,
and missing targets. A nonzero `Content-Length`, transfer encoding, or bytes
already trailing the parsed request head reject before route-specific handling.
Absent or zero `Content-Length` is admitted.

Every response closes its connection, so later bytes cannot become a second
routed request. The authenticated handshake likewise admits no request body.
Each of its six required version headers must appear exactly once: a 12-case
missing/duplicate matrix rejects malformed multiplicity with `400` before the
application compatibility service is invoked.

Header-name admission also probes canonical, all-lowercase, all-uppercase, and
every single-letter case flip for all six names. Each variant is equivalent when
unique and still rejects as a duplicate before application compatibility when
paired with the canonical name.

Automatic browser launch now fails startup closed before `ready`: the bound
loopback listener and fresh credential are released instead of leaving an
unreachable authenticated session running. The opener itself has one five-second
completion budget, so a hung platform helper is killed rather than holding the
process forever in `listening`. Recovery text is secret-free and directs a
restart after browser launch is fixed; it does not present the bare origin as an
authenticated continuation URL.

A real process fixture now records startup publication rather than inferring it
from source order: successful launch emits exactly `starting`, `listening`, then
`ready`, with no origin until the listener exists and no credential field in any
record. A deterministic failing opener emits only `starting` and `listening`,
exits nonzero, and keeps both the fragment and bound origin out of recovery
stderr. On Linux, absent or empty `DISPLAY` and `WAYLAND_DISPLAY` markers are
unavailable; an empty-marker process fixture still fails before `ready` even
when its fake opener would succeed.

The live Linux startup fixture also treats readiness text as untrusted:
only the exact five-field, secret-free `starting`, `listening`, `ready`
sequence establishes a session. The two latter records must publish the same
canonical IPv4 loopback origin and valid port. Red-first tests demonstrated
that a premature `ready`, inconsistent origins, wrong product/version, extra
credential fields, and malformed phase order previously passed the fixture.
This strengthens automation evidence, not the product's launcher handoff.

The opener process now receives null stdin, stdout, and stderr. Before that
change, a synthetic opener that echoed its URL copied the credential-bearing
fragment into both Atrament output streams; the same probe is now silent. A live
Linux `/proc` check also observed file descriptors 0, 1, and 2 all bound to
`/dev/null`.

Three hung-opener production trials failed closed in 5.003-5.007 seconds and
left no matching helper process. This closes child-output reflection and
unbounded launch waiting without claiming to solve the separate argv exposure
below.

The current automatic-launch handoff still passes the fragment-bearing URL as a
platform-opener command-line argument. A synthetic Linux probe observed that
argument, including its marker credential, through both `/proc/<pid>/cmdline`
and `ps` while the opener was alive. This violates the intended protection from
unrelated same-user local processes even though the fragment never enters an
HTTP request, log, query string, or persistent product file. P1 remains open
until the platform launcher uses a credential handoff that is not exposed via
child argv, environment, temporary files, or shell history; moving the same
secret between those surfaces is not an acceptable fix.

Request targets must use RFC 3986 origin-form path/query characters beginning
with `/`; absolute proxy form, `*`, fragments, controls, raw Unicode, and URI-
invalid visible punctuation reject before routing. Any `%` escape must carry
exactly two ASCII hexadecimal digits before routing.

An exhaustive raw graphic-ASCII oracle pins the admitted URI character classes,
and a 256-byte raw-target oracle additionally rejects every control and isolated
non-UTF-8 byte while admitting only the raw origin-form alphabet. A separate
16,384-pair ASCII oracle crosses every possible two-byte suffix after `%` and
admits exactly the 484 hexadecimal pairs, so percent-escape grammar no longer
depends on representative upper/lowercase examples.

Every header field name must also use the HTTP token grammar; whitespace,
Unicode, control characters, and other non-token names reject even when the
header is unrelated to Atrament. Header values reject control bytes other than
HTTP tab whitespace, including NUL and DEL, before routing. Exhaustive 128-byte
ASCII oracles independently pin both the complete field-name token alphabet and
the field-value control rule instead of relying only on sampled malformed
headers or generated request mutation.

Request framing rejects transfer encoding,
duplicate content lengths,
body-length mismatch, and non-digit Content-Length syntax such as a leading plus
sign before draft state can change. A complete 128-byte single-character
`Content-Length` oracle admits exactly the ten ASCII decimal digits with
matching body lengths and proves every other ASCII byte rejects without draft
mutation. A second full-ASCII composition oracle permits outer SP/HTAB or
another decimal digit around an existing zero while matching the resulting body
length; every other byte rejects. Same or conflicting duplicate lengths reject
regardless of header-name casing, including every ASCII case variant of the
second `Content-Length` field name.

Security-sensitive header values trim only HTTP space/tab OWS;
Unicode whitespace remains part of the value and therefore cannot normalize a
malformed Host, Origin, or Bearer credential into an admitted one. Full-ASCII
boundary oracles now verify outer Host, Origin, and Authorization normalization
byte by byte, and a separate 128-byte Bearer-separator oracle requires the
scheme's literal single ASCII space rather than treating tab or other whitespace
as equivalent. Exhaustive ASCII case-variant sweeps cover Host, Origin,
Authorization, Content-Length, and Transfer-Encoding field names; alternate
casing remains equivalent for admission, and case changes cannot hide duplicate
Host, Origin, or Authorization values.

Request-line, Host, Origin, and Bearer admission now decode only the complete
HTTP request head rather than the body. A 256-byte draft-body oracle requires
every possible single body byte to preserve valid security-header admission and
wrong-Host `421` precedence. At the correct Host, all 128 ASCII bytes remain
exact one-byte UTF-8 draft text while every isolated high-bit byte rejects with
`400`; invalid multi-byte UTF-8 also rejects without draft mutation.

Session-secret comparison executes all 64 admitted byte positions for every
candidate length;
0 through 128 byte candidates pin exact-length admission without claiming
platform-level constant-time execution.

Public, missing-route, unauthenticated, and authenticated empty responses are
also checked against exact session-credential reflection.

Current frontend evidence has no runtime dependencies, network client,
persistent browser storage, domain parser, external assets, referrer, autofill,
or browser spellcheck on session text. Its CSP blocks cross-origin script,
style, image, and fetch attempts before a second loopback origin receives a
request and denies unused frame, font, manifest, media, object, and worker
classes. Text, selection, and dynamic enabled-state restoration are disabled.
With the browser adapter active, every page exit clears all four session text
surfaces and their local enabled/count/copy state before navigation continues.

Fresh loads discard only URL fragments that resolve to local document IDs and
reset all three nested scrollports to their static origin. A 70-reload mixed
stress covered plain and percent-encoded local IDs plus unknown and malformed
fragments without a viewport-restoration failure.

Unknown fragments remain untouched by the presentation adapter. This avoids
preempting the runtime contract's in-memory session-secret handoff before the
authenticated startup adapter exists. It is an interim shell behavior, not the
final secret-handling guarantee: authenticated startup must consume an admitted
secret in memory and remove it from the visible URL.

Malformed or rejecting clipboard promises fail closed, and stale duplicate
completions cannot unlock a newer write. A checked-in real-Firefox stress
fixture
runs one thousand same-value prompt events plus one thousand concurrent repeat
clicks and retains exactly one active clipboard write, one pending-status
mutation, and no redundant disabled-state mutation before completion.

Prompt changes and page exit discard pending work and scrub the frontend's
prompt copy from an already-started request without claiming that the external
clipboard operation itself can be cancelled.

Before a bfcache snapshot, all four session text live values, default values,
and textarea text nodes are cleared. The disposable workspace then scrubs
retained descendant text and comment payloads plus detached attribute-node
values before removing its element tree.

A fixture retaining source, preview, page-stage, paper, diagnostic, textarea,
Text, Comment, and Attr references observed empty text and attribute payloads
after `pagehide(persisted=true)`. The textarea references also remained empty in
both live and default value state.

A real workspace → second-page → Back flow produced no intermediate workspace
request, then forced one fresh reload where those controls, counters, copy
status, and the three nested scrollports returned to their static pre-session
state. A 30-cycle bfcache stress repeated retained Text/Attr scrubbing and the
forced fresh reload without a scrub or restoration failure.

With bfcache disabled, the equivalent Back navigation performed a fresh
`back_forward` document load and again restored all four text surfaces empty and
all three nested scrollports to their static origin.

A Firefox crash-recovery fixture created live sessionstore recovery files, used
trusted keyboard input for task, source, and candidate text, presented a prompt,
then terminated Firefox with `SIGKILL`. Restarting the same profile restored the
workspace with all four surfaces empty and disabled and no plaintext secret hit
in the profile files.

Disposable viewport state follows the same rule. Reload, bfcache Back,
non-bfcache `back_forward`, and same-profile crash recovery all reset a 65/35
split, 160% preview zoom, and available nested scroll offsets to the static
46/54, 100%, and zero-origin state.

A control copy with `autocomplete="off"` removed only from Task restored its
secret after the same crash cycle, confirming that the fixture detects Firefox
form-state persistence rather than merely reopening a blank page.

The localhost runtime now emits response-level
`Content-Security-Policy: frame-ancestors 'none'; connect-src 'self'` and
`Referrer-Policy: no-referrer` on every response. Frame admission cannot be
restricted from the document-level meta CSP, while connection admission repeats
the shell's existing `connect-src 'self'` guard before its meta tag is parsed.
The referrer header also reinforces the shell's no-referrer declaration.

Backend fixtures pin these response headers on every served workspace resource
and across representative success, empty, authentication, conflict, routing,
and malformed-request response classes. A real Firefox fixture confirms a
CORS-permissive external loopback fetch succeeds from an unrestricted control.
It then rehosts the actual runtime CSP header on a meta-free document, proving
that the response header alone blocks the same fetch before any request arrives.
The original Atrament page also blocks that fetch while same-origin `/health`
remains accessible.

A real Firefox BiDi fixture now launches Atrament plus a hostile second loopback
origin. Direct top-level loading commits the workspace document, while framing
the same Atrament URL from that hostile origin commits Firefox's blocked
document instead. A permissive third loopback origin frames successfully from
the same attacker page, isolating Atrament's response policy rather than generic
cross-origin framing behavior. This proves browser enforcement of
`frame-ancestors 'none'`, not only header emission.

The same fixture performs document-initiated navigation from Atrament to a
hostile loopback recorder and observes no `Referer`, while a permissive control
document sends its ordinary origin referrer to that recorder. This proves
browser enforcement of `no-referrer`, not only header emission.

These browser constraints do not replace backend socket, token, host, path,
cleanup, framing, or hostile-origin acceptance tests.

The real-Firefox BiDi test harness now bounds startup-readiness output, TCP
socket establishment, and WebSocket upgrade-header reads. Each rejects early
stream close and timeouts without leaving stale listeners. Upgrade headers
are capped at 16 KiB, and fragmented responses preserve trailing WebSocket
frame bytes. A refused
upgrade explicitly destroys the client socket.

The harness also closes active HTTP fixture connections before awaiting server
shutdown, avoiding a test-cleanup dependency on a browser that still owns a
pending request. Independent Node fixtures exercise this shutdown against an
unfinished HTTP response and cover split upgrade headers and rejected
responses. These are reliability checks for the test harness, not changes to
the runtime's actual origin, credential, or frame-admission rules.

The BiDi command harness also clears each command deadline on both success
and protocol error, not only success. Focused framing fixtures exercise
successful responses, explicit protocol rejections, and finite unanswered
command deadlines without leaking pending-command map entries. These tests
bound automation state; they do not weaken or replace the backend's HTTP
request-admission or session-secret checks.

The real-Firefox request-observation helper now clears its five-second
deadline on early success or failure as well as expiration. A red-first
fake-timer regression reproduced the formerly retained timer and now checks
all three settlement paths. This is disposable fixture lifetime protection,
not a change to the production session request deadline.

BiDi upgrade acceptance now checks the exact `101` status, `Upgrade` and
`Connection` header values, unique header names, and the RFC 6455
`Sec-WebSocket-Accept` challenge response. It rejects a false `1010` status,
forged accept hashes, missing upgrade tokens and duplicate response headers.
The SHA-1 challenge is the standard WebSocket handshake proof, not application
request authentication and not an alternative to Atrament's session secret.

The disposable Firefox BiDi client now destroys its own socket on explicit
shutdown rather than half-closing the connection and relying on the browser
peer to finish. Peer close and error reject pending automation commands
immediately, and synchronous command-write failures clear command deadlines.

Partially initialized Firefox sessions close their BiDi connection when
startup fails. Unit regressions verify real TCP peer disconnect, idempotent
shutdown and failed writes. These are test-harness lifetime protections, not
changes to the backend localhost admission or runtime session policy.

Child-process teardown is now bounded after both graceful SIGTERM and forced
SIGKILL. The harness removes exit/error observers and cancels both deadlines
on normal exit or failure, rather than indefinitely awaiting a stubborn child.
Pure EventEmitter fixtures simulate immediate exit, escalation and a child
that never acknowledges either signal without launching an unrelated process.
These safeguards apply only to disposable browser-test child handles.

HTTP fixture teardown now has its own bounded deadline after closing active
ordinary HTTP connections. An independent upgraded-socket fixture proves that
Node cannot close every upgraded peer through `closeAllConnections`; if such a
peer prevents the server close callback, teardown reports a timeout rather than
blocking the test forever. The harness never claims to close arbitrary peers
or change runtime host/origin enforcement.

Inbound WebDriver BiDi framing now refuses an advertised message larger than
8 MiB, masked server frames, malformed JSON, null top-level messages and
explicit WebSocket close frames. A disconnect rejects every pending command,
and asynchronous notification history retains only the latest 16 events.
Independent frame fixtures cover 16-bit/64-bit lengths, fragmented messages,
oversized claims with no payload allocation, protocol errors and bounded
event history. These limits apply solely to disposable browser automation,
not to runtime session messages or public application protocol limits.

The separate real-runtime socket-confinement fixture now imposes the same
64 KiB total stdout ceiling. A red-first probe showed its readiness reader
could accumulate arbitrary bytes, throw from an untrusted JSON line directly
inside a stream event, or wait for timeout after a partial EOF. It now parses
bounded strictly decoded UTF-8, rejects malformed records with scrubbed
errors, handles stream end and error, and removes observers after settlement.
The production runtime's startup protocol and socket policy are unchanged.

Readiness output from spawned test processes now has a total 64 KiB UTF-8
byte ceiling and a finite deadline. A red-first fixture proved an unterminated
multi-byte stream could exhaust its deadline without bounded accumulation;
another showed the buffered child output appeared verbatim in readiness error
messages. Overflow now rejects and releases observers, and premature-close
or timeout errors do not reflect untrusted process output. This only protects
the browser test harness, not production runtime startup logs.

The Firefox harness now checks the endpoint printed on Firefox stderr
before opening a debugging socket: only an uncredentialed IPv4 loopback
`ws://127.0.0.1:<port>/session` endpoint is admitted. A red-first fixture
proved an off-host endpoint had previously reached client construction.
Non-loopback hosts, non-WebSocket schemes, URL credentials, query strings,
fragments, noncanonical aliases, and missing ports now fail before TCP connect.

Malformed endpoint errors also omit input rather than reflecting stderr data.
This protects the automation runner from misdirected debugging traffic; it
does not change the product's browser-launch credential exposure or listener.

The BiDi automation client now decodes WebSocket text with fatal UTF-8
validation before JSON parsing. A red-first fixture found that an invalid byte
in an otherwise valid command success previously became replacement text and
was accepted; the new single-frame and fragmented-byte regressions require
connection closure and pending-command rejection. Valid Unicode across
continuation frames remains supported, and this does not change the actual
localhost runtime's session transport.

The browser harness now assembles fragmented WebSocket text messages across
continuation frames under the same aggregate 8 MiB bound. It permits control
frames between fragments, responds to server Ping frames with masked Pong,
and refuses orphan continuations, overlapping text messages, binary data and
fragmented control frames. Synthetic fixtures verify exact Unicode response
bytes, split TCP headers, malformed sequencing and a synthetic over-limit
fragment total without allocating a giant input. This remains automation
framing only, not a production WebSocket or runtime protocol implementation.

The automation client also refuses RSV1, RSV2, and RSV3 frame flags because
its WebSocket upgrade negotiates no extensions. A red-first synthetic success
response showed those unnegotiated flags were previously ignored; all three
variants now close the peer and reject the pending command. This is a
WebDriver fixture invariant rather than a browser-runtime protocol change.

The same automation handshake now rejects extension and subprotocol offers
that its client never requested. Red-first fixtures also proved that the BiDi
frame parser accepted short JSON success messages encoded with extended 16-bit
or 64-bit length fields. It now requires the shortest permitted RFC 6455
length encoding before accepting payload bytes; valid extended frames still
assemble normally. These guards protect test evidence from malformed peers,
not production session transport or launcher credential handling.

Further red-first BiDi fixtures found that a malformed HTTP upgrade status
line (bare newline, tab or control byte) could pass as `101`, and a JSON
`success` with a missing, null, scalar or array result could complete a pending
command. The automation parser now requires the correct HTTP status-line
separators and an object-valued success result; Firefox's actual success
responses remain valid. This prevents malformed automation replies from
serving as readiness evidence without altering application protocol semantics.

BiDi command errors no longer echo complete JSON error envelopes into test
failures. A red-first fixture showed a remote peer could include private page
text in `message`, `stacktrace`, `data`, or even the error category, which the
client copied verbatim into the rejected command's Error.

Only recognized protocol error categories are now surfaced; unrecognized
categories become a constant diagnostic. The original command still fails and
clears its timer. This is automation log containment, not production browser
authentication.

WebSocket upgrade validation now also avoids assertion errors that embed a
remote status line, header token, or forged accept challenge. A red-first
fixture proved an untrusted peer could inject private marker text into the
assertion message and structured `actual` metadata. Static refusal messages
preserve every existing HTTP and RFC 6455 admission rule without echoing input.

The test harness also previously normalized malformed response header field
names and Unicode whitespace into valid upgrade authority. Red-first probes
cover leading/embedded name spaces, control bytes, bare newlines, and non-HTTP
whitespace. The parser now checks exact HTTP token names, refuses invalid
field-value controls, and trims only SP/HTAB on header values and tokens.
Ordinary Firefox upgrade headers remain accepted; this is harness hardening.

The real-runtime socket fixture also previously waited indefinitely after
SIGKILL if its child failed to exit. Red-first fake processes now cover an
unresponsive child, a failed signal, a thrown kill, and a delayed successful
exit. Teardown now applies a 10-second forced-reap budget and removes process
observers on every outcome without changing production shutdown semantics.

A full-browser stress run exposed that a 2-second forced-kill reap deadline
was too short under workstation memory pressure. The bounded SIGKILL window
is now 10 seconds, while a separate delayed-exit fixture proves successful
reaping after the earlier deadline. This retains a finite failure boundary
without misreporting slow child exit as an immediate test failure.

An automation-parser ceiling now caps WebDriver BiDi text fragments at
1,024, including zero-length continuations that consume no payload byte
budget. A focused fixture sends 1,025 fragments and verifies that the owned
socket and pending commands fail closed without unbounded fragment retention.

WebSocket upgrade-header scanning now inspects at most 16 KiB plus the
four-byte terminator before identifying the header boundary. A bounded frame
coalesced into the same TCP response is still preserved byte-for-byte, while
a similarly large unterminated header fails without copying the full chunk
into the header buffer. Both cases have dedicated regression tests.

A separate red-first fixture proved that a giant data frame sharing the
upgrade chunk was copied into a second unbounded buffer even though header
scanning was bounded. The reader now rejects a coalesced remainder larger
than the 8 MiB frame budget plus ten header bytes before concatenating data,
while the exact limit remains accepted. This protects disposable Firefox
harness memory, not the production runtime's HTTP request buffer.

A full-browser contention run reproduced intermittent Firefox readiness timeout
before an explicit-end assertion, while the same case passed in isolation.
The disposable test harness now permits 25 seconds for Firefox's BiDi startup
announcement and 50 seconds for each complete Firefox integration case.
These deadlines remain finite, apply only to tests, and do not change browser
session, backend readiness or real runtime admission policy.

A later high-contention full-browser pass exposed that Firefox could announce
its BiDi endpoint within the 25-second readiness window but still exceed a
separate 15-second `session.new` command deadline. The session handshake now
uses the same finite 25-second readiness allowance; a red-first fake BiDi
client fixture asserts both the handshake budget and connect-before-command
ordering without launching Firefox. The existing 50-second per-case ceiling
and actual browser/session transport admission remain unchanged.

Another full-browser contention run finished 206 of 207 cases but timed out
while Firefox created its first browsing context, before any divider key or
zoom assertion ran. The failing integration passed alone in about four
seconds. A red-first fake BiDi command fixture proved context creation still
had a 10-second default deadline while `session.new` had 25 seconds; only
`browsingContext.create` now uses that same finite 25-second startup budget.

Other commands retain ten seconds, explicit overrides still apply, and the
50-second case deadline remains intact. This is disposable test-harness
scheduling tolerance rather than a workspace runtime or security change.

A further high-contention run passed the previously failing divider case but
exposed an earlier startup seam: the Firefox WebSocket upgrade-header reader
still used its 10-second default and timed out before any session or private
handshake assertion. A red-first fake loopback debugger response now verifies
that the BiDi client uses the same 25-second finite readiness budget for its
upgrade. Low-level reader tests retain their explicitly shorter deadlines;
no product runtime network authority or retry behavior changes.

The subsequent high-contention full Firefox run reached 210 of 211 cases,
but the direct workspace tab's first DOM read was blank immediately after
WebDriver BiDi reported a completed navigation. The actual failure was not the
blocked hostile iframe.

A red-first fixture now supplies 21 transient blank
document reads before the expected Atrament title and workspace structure,
and separately requires a persistent unrelated document to fail closed.
Only this direct-workspace readiness observation gains up to 40 bounded
quarter-second polls. The framing and CSP assertions remain unchanged; this
is test-harness scheduling tolerance, not a product policy relaxation.

A temporary one-line relaxation of the runtime HTTP header to
`frame-ancestors *` makes the same real Firefox fixture fail at the
hostile iframe content assertion. The original runtime source was
restored byte-for-byte after this mutation proof.

Client-side session calls now have a six-second abort deadline spanning both
`fetch()` and any valid-media response-body stream. A stream that sends a
partial compatible handshake, text draft, or `413` diagnostic and never closes
could previously hold browser controls indefinitely despite bounded bytes.
Red-first Node fixtures verify that the incomplete content never becomes
accepted authority; stalled draft limit diagnostics retain an explicit retry
rather than being mistaken for malformed backend diagnostics.

A real Firefox fixture observes closure of all three stalled sockets, no
partial draft hydration, same-tab recovery, and a successful later draft edit
retry. The
explicit End session request has the same finite deadline and does not claim
backend erasure if its outcome remains unknown. This is browser lifetime
hardening, not proof of backend operation cancellation or media-file cleanup.

A separate Firefox fixture now stalls the explicit End session response before
sending any headers, with no response body to decode. The six-second client
request deadline aborts that connection; the UI preserves the private draft
and reports failure instead of claiming erasure. After the same-tab user
confirms End again, a valid response clears session authority and draft text.
This verifies client retry semantics, not backend destruction after an unknown
prior request outcome.

The Firefox automation readiness reader now consumes raw process bytes with a
fatal streaming UTF-8 decoder, rather than accepting replacement characters
from malformed startup output. Red-first fixtures proved invalid output could
previously be selected as a readiness marker; tests now reject both malformed
raw bytes and an already-decoded text-mode stream while preserving valid
multibyte output split at every byte.

A separate red-first fixture proved that unsuccessful `SIGTERM` and
`SIGKILL` returns were ignored by Firefox fixture
teardown. Those failures now reject promptly, clear process observers, and
cancel both shutdown deadlines. Neither change alters the actual browser
launcher or resolves its launch URL credential exposure.

Browser media-type admission now trims only HTTP's ASCII outer optional
whitespace (space and horizontal tab), rather than JavaScript's wider Unicode
whitespace set. Red-first Node regressions found that NBSP, U+FEFF, vertical
tab, and line breaks could previously turn malformed handshake, draft-read,
and draft-diagnostic content types into accepted media declarations. The
corrected paths retain case-insensitive matching and valid ASCII outer OWS.
This narrows browser response metadata admission; it does not authenticate a
misrouted response or alter backend media validation.

The locked `nix` filesystem feature also exposes `fcntl::openat` and
`fcntl::openat2`, whose writable or creation flags are chosen at each call.
The previous mutation scanner admitted those imports in unrelated modules
because its direct-function list focused on explicit writes and renames.

A separate source inventory now confines every `nix::fcntl` use, including
nested/grouped imports, aliases, and raw Rust identifiers, to the reviewed
selected-profile file-open and explicit file-commit adapters. Five injected
unreviewed declarations or calls in `session-secret` each failed that guard;
the production file was restored byte-for-byte. Read-only imports of other
`nix` namespaces remain admitted.

This is source-level review evidence, not
runtime syscall confinement or proof of safe flags inside the reviewed
adapters. The P8 end-to-end security and privacy task remains open.

Another source-level filesystem check covers `std::fs::DirBuilder`, whose
`create` method can create directories without calling `fs::create_dir`.
The locked `nix` stat module also exposes `mknod` and `mknodat` for file
creation, even when callers rename those imported functions.

The direct filesystem authority inventory now includes these constructors,
import aliases, nested imports, and raw-identifier spellings. Five temporary
`session-secret` source mutations were rejected and restored byte-for-byte.
The pinned Rust compiler accepted an actual `DirBuilder` creation expression.
This does not establish runtime write isolation or close the P8 task.

The pinned stable Rust standard library also exposes Unix `chown`,
`lchown`, `fchown`, and `chroot`. These can change file ownership or the
process root without calling an ordinary `std::fs` write function.
The source guard now includes direct and renamed imports, nested imports,
raw identifiers, and aliases of the Unix filesystem module.

Five temporary `session-secret` source mutations each failed the updated
inventory and were restored byte-for-byte. The previous committed scanner
accepted an aliased Unix `chown` import that the updated scanner rejects.
The pinned Rust compiler accepted all four stable Unix API calls. This is
source inventory only, not filesystem access control or a closed P8 task.

Process-global pathname changes also affect the selected CWD and export
anchors, so a separate zero-owner inventory now checks `std::env`
`set_current_dir` and locked `nix` `chdir`, `fchdir`, `chroot`, and `umask`.
It rejects direct calls, grouped and renamed imports, raw identifiers, and
module aliases while continuing to admit read-only environment and stat
queries.

Six injected changes in the unreviewed `session-secret` module each failed;
the exact source bytes were restored afterward. The previous source guard
admitted an injected `std::env::set_current_dir` call; the new one fails.
This review gate does not prove syscall-level confinement or close the
release security task.

Process-wide environment changes can redirect the browser opener through
`PATH` or alter inherited GUI session markers without calling a process or
filesystem API directly. A red-first source regression now requires review
of `std::env::set_var`, `remove_var`, renamed imports, raw identifiers, and
module aliases, while leaving environment reads admitted.

Three deliberately inactive `session-secret` source mutations each failed the
new zero-owner inventory and were restored byte-for-byte. The previous path
scanner admitted `std::env::set_var`; the new guard refuses it. This is
static source review, not assurance about external launcher environments,
indirect native mutations, or the unresolved opener-argv credential leak.

A further red-first process inventory closes delegated launch calls that need
no direct `std::process` reference in the invoking backend module. It now
reviews `.spawn`, `.status`, `.output`, `.exec`, `pre_exec`, and `before_exec`
methods, Unix/Windows `CommandExt`, and imports or aliases of parent
platform-process namespaces. Read-only `ExitStatusExt` imports remain allowed.

A compile-only pinned Rust fixture accepted a module that imports only Unix
`CommandExt` and an application-supplied `Command` type before calling
`exec` and `spawn`. The previous scanner admitted the method call, while
the new cases require explicit process-owner review. This remains source
inventory rather than complete Rust name resolution, runtime spawn
confinement, or a remedy for the opener-argv credential leak.

Three inactive `session-secret` mutations (a Unix `CommandExt` import,
a platform-module alias, and a delegated `spawn` call) each failed the
unreviewed-process inventory. The original backend source was restored
byte-for-byte after every probe. No production process-launch code changed.

The backend authority scanner now also validates Cargo workspace target source
paths, not only dependency versions and build-script presence. A new `lib` or
`bin` target could otherwise compile Rust outside the reviewed `src/backend`
walk; `[[test]]` roots are separately confined to `tests/backend`. The guard
resolves `..` segments and filesystem symlinks before admitting each target,
rejecting missing files, non-Rust targets, and unexpected Cargo target kinds.

Synthetic target metadata verifies that a library or binary redirected to a
backend test source is refused, while ordinary source roots and Cargo's
existing relative test paths remain admitted. The source walker additionally
refuses symbolic-link entries, which could otherwise hide imported module
files outside its directory recursion. The locked workspace's 132 library,
one binary, and 131 external test targets pass this inventory. This is
build-source confinement evidence, not a native execution sandbox, validation
of arbitrary build tool behavior, or a fix for opener-argv credential exposure.

The pinned Rust standard library also exposes stable Windows filesystem APIs
`std::os::windows::fs::symlink_file`, `symlink_dir`, and
`FileExt::seek_write`. These create links or write through already-open file
handles without calling the Unix positional-write or generic `std::fs`
functions. The prior source detector admitted `symlink_file`; a red-first
regression now requires review of both Windows link constructors and
positional writes, while retaining read-only `seek_read` admission.

Three inactive `session-secret` source mutations using direct or nested
Windows link imports and a delegated `seek_write` each failed the new
inventory and were restored byte-for-byte. The pinned Rust 1.97.1 std source
confirms these APIs are stable; no Windows runtime test was performed. This
is source-level filesystem mutation review, not Windows syscall confinement
or proof of the overall release privacy boundary.

A Firefox localhost policy fixture intermittently timed out its initial
`browsingContext.navigate` command after ten seconds, before policy
assertions, yet passed in isolation. A red-first fake-timer regression now
pins the finite 25-second Firefox startup budget for tab navigation as well
as creation, without extending the normal 10-second BiDi command deadline.
The exact policy fixture passes with the revised harness budget; this changes
no production runtime behavior or browser security policy.

Compile-time `include_bytes!`, `include_str!`, `env!`, and `option_env!`
can embed unrelated local files or environment values into the executable
without making a runtime filesystem call. An exact source inventory now
admits only the seven existing browser workspace assets and the backend's
`CARGO_PKG_VERSION` option, rejecting new input paths, macros, aliases,
and dynamic macro arguments pending explicit privacy review.

Two inactive `session-secret` source mutations embedding a host file or the
`HOME` environment variable each failed this inventory and were restored
byte-for-byte. The earlier source inclusion guard accepted those embeddings.
This is a compile-time input review gate, not a guarantee against external
build behavior, opaque dependencies, or the browser-opener argv leak.

The backend console is a second privacy boundary: an unreviewed print macro
or stderr handle could reveal session text without any network or file-open
API. A red-first source inventory now refuses print, eprint, and debug
macros, imported aliases, any stderr access, and additional stdout access
beyond the bootstrap's two existing marker writes.

Two inactive `session-secret` mutations adding `eprintln!` or a direct
stderr handle failed this new source guard, then were restored byte-for-byte.
This pins declared Rust output paths for review, not the behavior of opaque
dependencies, native syscalls, or subprocesses. It does not address the
known browser-opener argv exposure.

The backend console is a second privacy boundary: an unreviewed print macro
or stderr handle could reveal session text without any network or file-open
API. A red-first source inventory now refuses print, eprint, and debug
macros, imported aliases, any stderr access, and additional stdout access
beyond the bootstrap's two existing marker writes.

Two inactive `session-secret` mutations adding `eprintln!` or a direct
stderr handle failed this new source guard, then were restored byte-for-byte.
This pins declared Rust output paths for review, not the behavior of opaque
dependencies, native syscalls, or subprocesses. It does not address the
known browser-opener argv exposure.

The backend console is a second privacy boundary: an unreviewed print macro
or stderr handle could reveal session text without any network or file-open
API. A red-first source inventory now refuses print, eprint, and debug
macros, imported aliases, any stderr access, and additional stdout access
beyond the bootstrap's two existing marker writes.

Two inactive `session-secret` mutations adding `eprintln!` or a direct
stderr handle failed this new source guard, then were restored byte-for-byte.
This pins declared Rust output paths for review, not the behavior of opaque
dependencies, native syscalls, or subprocesses. It does not address the
known browser-opener argv exposure.

Direct ambient filesystem reads need privacy review even when no write or
network API is visible in the module. A red-first backend source regression
now catches `File::open`, `std::fs::read`, `read_to_string`, `read_dir`, and
`read_link`, including grouped or renamed imports and raw identifiers. Only
the four existing profile target, commit, selected-open, and import adapters
currently own these direct read paths.

Three inactive source mutations in the unreviewed `session-secret` adapter
tried file read, directory enumeration, and direct file open. All failed the
new inventory and were restored byte-for-byte. Read-only metadata lookups
remain permitted. This guards reviewed source declarations, not indirect
filesystem access, native calls, or the unresolved opener-argv credential.

Runtime environment values may contain unrelated host credentials despite
requiring no file-open or network API to read them. A red-first source guard
now admits only the two existing GUI-session marker reads, `DISPLAY` and
`WAYLAND_DISPLAY`, in the browser launcher. Other `std::env` value and
iterator reads, including direct, grouped, and aliased imports, require
privacy review. CLI argument inspection remains a distinct reviewed path.

Two inactive `session-secret` mutations requesting a private environment
value or importing an environment iterator each failed the updated guard.
The backend source was restored byte-for-byte. This is static declaration
evidence, not proof against native environment access or inherited opener
state, and the known credential-bearing opener argv remains unresolved.

Rust permits comments as lexical whitespace inside paths. Before this fix,
`use std/**/::net::TcpStream` and a nested-comment process import escaped
the backend source inventories, despite compiling under the pinned Rust
1.97.1 toolchain. A red-first scanner fixture reproduced both misses.

The source review now removes line and nested block comments while retaining
normal, byte, and raw string literals, before normalizing Rust raw identifiers.
Its source-inclusion guard uses the same normalization so commented macro
separators cannot redirect compilation without review.

Three separate inactive `session-secret` mutations with comment-separated
network, process, and environment imports each failed the updated inventory.
All source bytes were restored afterward; the scanner passes 36 cases. This
is still a conservative source gate, not a complete Rust parser or syscall
sandbox, and it does not resolve the browser-opener argv credential leak.

The host CLI argv can also contain explicitly selected private paths and
untrusted user input. A red-first source test showed that a new
`env::args_os()` call in `session-secret` escaped the prior environment-only
inventory. Only `env::args_os().skip(1)` in runtime bootstrap is now admitted.

Three inactive backend mutations introduced a direct argv read, a renamed
argument iterator, and an `std::env::*` wildcard import. All three fail the
source inventory and were restored byte-for-byte. This gates new declared
argument readers but does not hide process arguments from the operating
system or resolve the platform opener's known credential-bearing argv.

Standard input is another inherited host boundary. Unlike CLI argument
inspection, `std::io::stdin()` can read arbitrary bytes passed to a process.
A red-first test showed that the prior stdout/stderr disclosure guard admitted
new stdin reads from unreviewed backend modules.

The standard-stream source guard now rejects direct, grouped, aliased, and raw
identifier stdin uses while retaining the two reviewed CLI status writes.
Three cfg-disabled `session-secret` source mutations using stdin handles and
imports each failed the updated scanner before byte-for-byte restoration.
This is static source review, not an operating-system sandbox, and does not
resolve the known browser-opener credential in child argv.

Host path discovery exposes process working directories, executable paths,
and temporary or home directories without opening a file. A red-first
source fixture showed that `std::env::current_dir()` and imported
`temp_dir` aliases evaded the previous environment/argv source inventory.

The ambient-input review now covers `current_dir`, `current_exe`, `home_dir`,
and `temp_dir`, including renamed or grouped imports and raw identifiers.
Three inactive backend mutations for working-directory, temporary-directory,
and executable-path reads failed the new guard and were restored exactly.
No production host-path readers are admitted; source review does not prove
absence of native or indirect path discovery or fix the opener argv leak.

Host filesystem metadata and path resolution can also reveal file existence,
identity, or the resolved target of a caller-supplied path. A red-first scan
showed that `std::fs::metadata`, `symlink_metadata`, and `canonicalize` were
previously excluded from the direct-file intake inventory.

These named direct functions and their grouped or aliased imports now require
review. The existing Export session's current-directory identity check is
explicitly admitted alongside the four previously reviewed profile adapters.
Three inactive `session-secret` mutations using direct metadata, an alias,
and nested canonicalization imports failed before byte-for-byte restoration.
This is source-level evidence, not a proof about indirect `Path` methods or
native metadata system calls; the opener argv leak remains outstanding.

The live Linux socket-confinement fixture now also inventories `/proc` task
IDs at readiness, while a public request is held open, and throughout the
authenticated handshake and private-draft read/write sequence. At each
observation, the runtime owns exactly its main thread, and no persistent
background thread appears alongside the reviewed loopback sockets.

This is live-process evidence during actual private text use, not a source
inventory or proof that a short-lived thread could never appear between
samples. The synthetic opener still carries the test URL over IPC, and the
production browser-opener argv credential disclosure remains unresolved.

The live Linux one-thread snapshots cannot exclude workers created between
observations or introduced later. A red-first source mutation added a
`std::thread::spawn` call under an inactive backend configuration; the
previous 36-case privacy suite passed despite that unreviewed authority.

The existing process-owner source review now also covers direct and renamed
Rust thread spawn entrypoints, grouped `std::thread` imports, thread builders,
and delegated scoped or unchecked spawn methods. Read-only thread sleeps in
the browser-launch adapter remain admitted. Four inactive `session-secret`
mutations were rejected by the new owner inventory and restored byte-for-byte.
This is a source review gate, not a kernel-level thread prohibition, and it
neither establishes temporal runtime confinement nor fixes opener argv leakage.

Path and file metadata methods can probe host files or resolve symlinks
without the direct `std::fs` calls covered by the earlier inventory. A
red-first inactive `Path::exists` addition in `session-secret` passed the old
36-case privacy suite; the pinned Rust compiler independently accepts the
stable Path metadata, read-directory, and link-query methods.

The source review now also covers method calls and associated-function calls
for filesystem existence, identity, metadata, directory listing, and symlink
resolution, including raw identifiers and aliased `std::path` imports. The
existing selected-file reader is an explicitly reviewed metadata owner, along
with the prior profile adapters. Four inactive backend probes for existence,
read-link, grouped aliases, and commented/raw path syntax failed and were
restored byte-for-byte.

This is a conservative source review, not type-based proof of actual file
access or protection against native syscalls. The browser opener's
credential-bearing argv remains unresolved.

Inherited raw file descriptors can disclose host input or act as filesystem
or socket authority even when no new `std::fs::open`, `stdin`, or network
constructor is present. The pinned Rust compiler accepts unsafe
`File::from_raw_fd(0)`, which can adopt standard input as a File.
An inactive backend `FromRawFd` import and constructor passed the earlier
36-case privacy inventory before the new review gate was added.

A zero-owner source scanner now rejects raw FD, socket, and native handle
adoption constructors and imports, including owned and borrowed wrappers,
renamed namespaces, grouped imports, and raw identifiers. Ordinary `AsRawFd`,
`AsFd`, and raw-descriptor observation remain admitted. Four temporary backend
mutations were rejected by the new guard and restored byte-for-byte.

This is static authority review, not a runtime FD isolation sandbox or a
proof against arbitrary unsafe casts. The browser-opener argv credential
exposure remains open.

A caller-provided File or socket can be written through `std::io::copy`
without importing the `Write` trait or calling a `.write` method in the
current module. The pinned compiler accepts an in-memory Cursor copied into
a borrowed File; an inactive backend source mutation of that call passed the
prior 38-case security inventory.

The stream-writing owner inventory now also reviews direct and grouped
`std::io::copy` imports and calls, including raw identifiers, plus delegated
`flush` methods that could persist caller-owned buffered output. Four
inactive backend mutations were rejected and restored byte-for-byte.
The existing bootstrap, loopback response, and explicit profile commit
remain the only admitted stream-writing owners.

This source-level contract cannot prove the concrete destination type or
runtime persistence absence. The browser-opener argv credential exposure
remains unresolved.

The pinned Rust 1.97.1 standard library also exposes `std::fs::exists`,
which queries private host path existence without opening a file. The prior
ambient filesystem review detected `Path::exists` but admitted a direct
`std::fs::exists` call. A red-first detector test reproduced that gap; the
read-side authority inventory now reviews direct, renamed, grouped, and
raw-identifier `fs::exists` imports. Four inactive `session-secret` source
mutations were rejected, then restored byte-for-byte.

This remains a source-level privacy review gate, not a sandbox for indirect
metadata syscalls or a remedy for the browser-opener argv credential
exposure.

The `include!` macro can pull unreviewed Rust into a backend module despite
all tracked `.rs` files passing the source inventory. The pinned Rust compiler
accepts braces and bracket-with-semicolon forms, as well as imports of the
macro under another name from `std` or `core`. The previous redirect scanner
only matched parenthesized `include!`, while the crate-root alias guard only
rejected renaming `std`.

Red-first regressions now reject all three `include!` delimiters, macro
imports from either standard-library root, and renamed `core` root imports.
Seven inactive backend source mutations were rejected, then restored
byte-for-byte.

These are static source inclusion checks, not a substitute for compiler
expansion auditing, syscall confinement, or the unresolved browser-opener
argv credential handoff.

The stable `std::path::absolute` function can resolve a relative path using
the host process CWD without an explicit `std::env::current_dir` call in the
importing module. The previous path-read scanner admitted this indirect host
path discovery. A red-first regression now requires direct, grouped, renamed,
and raw-identifier imports of `path::absolute` to undergo privacy review.

Four inactive source mutations of the unprivileged `session-secret` adapter
were rejected by the updated inventory and restored byte-for-byte. Purely
lexical `Path::is_absolute` remains outside that review. This static source
check does not prove native call confinement or fix opener argv exposure.

Pinned `nix` 0.31.3 with its `fs` feature exposes additional ambient path
queries through `unistd::access`, `faccessat`, and `getcwd`, metadata probes
through `sys::stat`, and link-target reads through `fcntl::readlink`.
The previous standard-library path inventory did not detect those direct
calls or renamed nested imports, allowing an unreviewed backend module to
probe host path permissions, metadata, or the working directory.

Red-first source regressions now confine those queries to the same reviewed
profile adapters as standard-library path readers. Seven inactive
`session-secret` source mutations were rejected and restored byte-for-byte.
Read-only `nix::unistd::read`, `nix::sys::stat::Mode`, and
`nix::fcntl::openat2` remain outside this specific query inventory; their
separate authority rules continue to apply.

An already-open File or socket can also be read through `std::io::Read`
without an `fs::open`, `File::open`, or `stdin()` call in the consuming
module. The prior 38-case backend inventory admitted a disabled
`File::read` call on a borrowed descriptor. The new stream-reading review
guards direct and aliased `Read`/`BufRead` imports, borrowed stream methods,
and Unix/Windows positional `FileExt` readers.

The reviewed reader owners are the profile file reader, bounded profile
entry stream reader, explicit profile file-commit readback, and loopback
HTTP runtime. Seven inactive `session-secret` stream-read mutations were
rejected and restored byte-for-byte. This is a static owner inventory, not a
proof of descriptor provenance or a remedy for the opener argv leak.

Stable `Read::bytes` and `BufRead::lines` turn borrowed file input into
iterators, so `.next()` consumes file bytes without a local `.read()` call or
trait import. A pinned Rust compile probe confirmed those fully qualified
calls; an inactive source injection passed the former 40-case scanner.

The stream-reading review now also checks fully qualified iterator adapters
such as `bytes`, `lines`, `split`, `take`, `chain`, and `by_ref`. Four inactive
backend mutations were rejected, then restored byte-for-byte. Unrelated
in-memory `str::bytes` remains permitted; this static policy is not a
runtime descriptor-provenance proof.

Rust file constructors are first-class function items: a backend module can
capture `File::open` or `File::create`, then invoke the local binding without
an immediate `open(` or `create(` token. The former read-side and write-side
source detectors missed those forms, while a renamed `std::fs` module or
`std::fs::File` type could also conceal a host file read. Red-first tests
reproduced both gaps; pinned Rust 1.97.1 accepted the aliased constructor,
`create_new`, `options`, and permission-setting method items.

The source inventory now reviews these file operations even when referenced as
function items and treats renamed standard filesystem modules and file types
as requiring host-read review. Five inactive `session-secret` source probes
were rejected and restored byte-for-byte, including two read constructors,
two alias forms, and writable file or permission function items. This remains
static source review, not a type-resolved filesystem sandbox, and does not
address the known browser-opener credential exposure in process arguments.

Rust `type` aliases retain the associated functions of filesystem types. A
module can alias `std::fs::File` or an imported `File` and invoke
`HostFile::open` or `HostFile::create` without the original `File::` token.
Aliases of `Path`, `PathBuf`, and `DirEntry` likewise hide UFCS path queries.
Red-first read and write authority tests reproduced both omissions, and the
pinned Rust 1.97.1 compiler admitted all three File aliases and associated
open/create/options function items.

The static backend source review now flags filesystem `type` aliases even when
only an unqualified standard type is named, while unrelated crate-local types
and metadata aliases remain permitted. Five inactive `session-secret` probes
covering File, Path, DirEntry, raw identifiers, and Unicode alias names were
rejected, then removed with exact original backend bytes restored. This is
conservative source gating without Rust type resolution, not a guarantee
against dynamically constructed native authority or the browser-opener argv
credential exposure.

The locked nix 0.31.3 `fs` dependency also exposes `nix::unistd::read` on any
borrowed `AsFd`, without importing `std::io::Read` or opening a path in the
consuming module. The prior source inventory admitted a direct borrowed-File
read in an unreviewed backend adapter.

A red-first regression now covers direct, renamed, grouped, raw-identifier, and
whole-module nix read imports under the same four-owner stream-reading review.
Six cfg-inactive mutations of the selected-profile opener, which already
depends on nix, were rejected before restoring its original bytes exactly. This
is static source review, not runtime descriptor provenance, syscall
confinement, or a remedy for the browser-opener argv credential exposure.

The pinned nix 0.31.3 `fs` feature also exposes descriptor duplication and
standard-stream redirection through `dup`, `dup2_*`, and `dup3` functions.
These can copy inherited file descriptors without close-on-exec or rebind
process-wide stdin, stdout, and stderr from an existing `AsFd`, without a
`std::io` or `std::process` import. The earlier zero-owner standard-stream
policy admitted `nix::unistd::dup2_stdout(&file)`.

Red-first source regressions now review direct, grouped, renamed,
raw-identifier, and whole-module nix duplication imports as unreviewed
standard-stream authority. Eight disabled mutations in the selected-profile
opener, a real nix dependency owner, were rejected and the file restored
byte-for-byte. No backend module currently owns descriptor rebinding. This is
conservative source review, not a kernel-enforced descriptor policy or a fix
for the browser-launch argv credential leak.

The pinned Rust 1.97.1 compiler permits `Path::exists`, `Path::canonicalize`,
`File::metadata`, and `DirEntry::metadata` as first-class function items. A
module can store one and later call its binding without the original method
name followed by `(`, bypassing the prior host-path read source detector. A
red-first scanner regression reproduced this omission.

The path-read inventory now also reviews inherited filesystem metadata and path
query method items, including raw-identifier spellings; unrelated lexical
methods and file synchronization remain outside this rule. Seven cfg-inactive
source mutations in the unreviewed `session-secret` adapter failed the updated
guard, and its original bytes were restored exactly. This does not type-resolve
method receivers, enforce syscalls, or remedy the browser opener argv
credential exposure.

Rust 1.97.1 accepts first-class `Read` and `Write` trait method items,
including `<File as std::io::Read>::read_to_end` and `<File as
std::io::Write>::write_all`, which can later consume or mutate a borrowed File
with no corresponding method-call token in the source module. It also accepts
`Read::bytes` and `BufRead::lines` as stored iterator adapters. The previous
stream inventories overlooked these item references without adjacent
parentheses or a local trait import.

Red-first detector tests now require reviewed reader/writer ownership for
direct and fully qualified stream method items, including generic reader types,
raw identifiers, and captured iterator adapters. Ten cfg-inactive
`session-secret` source mutations were rejected and byte-restored (five
borrowed-read items, three stream-write items, and two iterator variants). This
static rule does not resolve actual receiver types, enforce descriptor
provenance, or address the browser opener argv leak.

The pinned Rust 1.97.1 compiler accepts fully qualified Unix `FileExt`
`write_at` and `write_all_at` method items on `File`, even when no positional
write call appears in the module. A red-first mutation-detector test exposed
that the previous filesystem write review missed these captured items. The
review now checks direct and fully qualified positional write items, including
Windows `seek_write` source syntax, raw identifiers, and renamed `FileExt`
imports that could hide later method references. Existing anonymous `as _`
imports used solely for read extension methods remain admitted; their write
calls are still scanned.

Five cfg-inactive `session-secret` mutations (qualified writes, renamed or
nested trait imports, and comment-separated raw identifiers) failed the
updated single-owner filesystem mutation inventory and were byte-restored.
The scanner does not resolve trait types, prove descriptor provenance, or
address the known browser-opener argv credential disclosure.

The pinned Rust 1.97.1 compiler also accepts environment and argv readers as
first-class function items: `env::var::<&str>`, `current_dir`, and `args_os`
can be captured and invoked later. Likewise, the unsafe `set_var` and
`remove_var` function items need no immediate call token. The existing
source inventories reviewed imports and calls but overlooked these bare
references, allowing a disabled backend probe to bypass source review.

A red-first regression now reviews function-item references for environment,
host-path, and argv reads, and keeps the exact browser GUI-marker and bootstrap
argv-reader counts intact. The zero-owner process-environment mutation guard
also rejects qualified mutator items, including raw identifiers, without
flagging crate-local `env` modules. Eight cfg-inactive backend mutations across
`session-secret`, browser launch, and runtime bootstrap were rejected and all
original bytes restored. These are static checks, not OS-level environment
isolation, and the browser-opener argv credential issue remains unresolved.

Scoped Rust threads can execute host code without a conventional
`thread::spawn` call in the declaring module. The pinned Rust 1.97.1 compiler
accepts `Scope::spawn` captured as a first-class method item and later invoked
through a local function binding. A red-first scanner fixture showed that a
captured `std::thread::scope` also bypassed the prior process authority review.

The process owner inventory now reviews direct and imported `thread::scope`,
`thread::Scope`, and captured `Scope::spawn` or `Builder::spawn_scoped` items,
including grouped aliases and raw identifiers. Six cfg-inactive
`session-secret` mutations were rejected before exact restoration of the
original module bytes. This does not prove that short-lived threads never
exist between process snapshots, and does not resolve browser opener argv
credential exposure.

The pinned Rust compiler accepts `std::os::unix::process::CommandExt` as a
fully qualified trait bound, with `T::exec`, `T::pre_exec`, and deprecated
`T::before_exec` captured as method items. The earlier process scanner checked
imports and immediate platform extension calls, but missed the direct generic
bound and bare `CommandExt::exec` items. A red-first regression reproduced the
miss without any new registry dependencies or `std::process` references.

The process authority inventory now reviews directly qualified Unix and
Windows `CommandExt` paths and captured `CommandExt` launch method items.
Four disabled source injections using a direct trait bound, an absolute root,
raw identifiers, and comment-separated syntax were refused, then the
`session-secret` source was restored byte-for-byte. Read-only platform
`ExitStatusExt` references stay admitted. This is conservative static review,
not a runtime process sandbox or a fix for the browser-opener credential
exposure through child arguments.

Rust `std::process::Command` also exposes `new`, `spawn`, `status`, and
`output` as first-class function items: construction or process execution can
happen later through a local binding, without a nearby `Command::new(...)` or
`.spawn()` call. The pinned Rust 1.97.1 compiler accepts all four forms.
The red-first fixture confirmed that the former process authority scanner
missed a captured `Command::spawn` function item.

A red-first regression now reviews those captured constructor and execution
items, along with extension-method items on `Command`, without requiring
an adjacent call. Six disabled mutations covering the four compiler-proven
functions, raw identifiers, and comment-separated syntax failed the guard;
the backend bytes were restored exactly. Unrelated `Command::preview` and
read-only process status extension imports remain outside this conservative
review. This is static source evidence, not an execution sandbox or a repair
for the browser-opener credential exposed in child argv.

The pinned `nix` 0.31.3 `fs` feature also enables `unistd::mkstemp`,
which creates a persistent regular file from a template and returns an open
file descriptor. The former direct filesystem mutation inventory reviewed
`nix::fcntl` open paths and many `unistd` mutators, but missed this constructor.
Rust 1.97.1 compiled a captured `nix::unistd::mkstemp::<str>` function item;
`unistd::mkdtemp` is **not** enabled by the pinned feature set.

A red-first regression demonstrated the missing `mkstemp` authority. The
updated source inventory now detects direct and captured calls, grouped and
renamed imports, raw identifiers, and comment-separated paths. Four inactive
`session-secret` mutations failed the strengthened filesystem owner check;
each mutation was restored byte-for-byte.

No production code changed. This source review does not prove syscall
isolation, clean up temporary files, or resolve the browser opener's
exposed credential in child argv.

The pinned `nix` 0.31.3 `fs` feature exposes `sys::statfs` and
`sys::statvfs` to query mounted filesystem capacity, type and identity by
path or inherited descriptor. These APIs were absent from the narrower
`sys::stat`/`fcntl` ambient-host-read inventory. Rust 1.97.1 compiled all
four function-item forms (`statfs`, `fstatfs`, `statvfs`, `fstatvfs`).

The red-first read test exposed this gap; the strengthened inventory now
reviews direct references and nested, renamed or raw-identifier module imports.
Seven inactive `session-secret` probes failed the owner check, and every
injected source was restored byte-for-byte. No production adapter gained
ambient filesystem statistics authority.

This remains a static source-review gate, not runtime syscall confinement.
It does not resolve the known session credential exposure in browser-opener
argv or certify all possible host filesystem information channels.

The pinned `nix` `sys::sysinfo` module is enabled on Linux without the
optional `signal` or `process` features. It returns machine load averages,
uptime, process count and RAM/swap totals without reading environment,
filesystem paths or standard input. The prior ambient-input inventory
missed this host-status source; Rust 1.97.1 compiled its function item.

A red-first regression now requires review of direct calls, captured items,
grouped module aliases, raw identifiers and comment-separated imports.
Five disabled `session-secret` probes failed the updated input-owner check;
all temporary source bytes were restored exactly. No backend module owns
ambient host machine-status reads, and no production code changed.

This is an inventory for explicit privacy review, not runtime prevention
of hardware fingerprinting, native syscalls, or the existing browser-opener
argv credential disclosure.

The locked nix 0.31.3 `fs` feature also exposes Linux
`nix::sys::memfd::memfd_create`. It creates a mutable, anonymous
memory-backed file descriptor; unlike explicit profile export it has no
selected path, and without `MFD_CLOEXEC` the descriptor can survive process
execution. A Rust 1.97.1 compile-only function-item fixture confirmed the API
is available under the locked dependency features.

A red-first source regression showed that an unreviewed memory-file creator
previously escaped the filesystem authority inventory. The detector now
reviews direct calls, captured function items, renamed and grouped imports,
raw identifiers, and comment-separated paths under the existing explicit
profile-output owner policy. Seven inactive `session-secret` source injections
were rejected individually and restored byte-for-byte. This does not prove
runtime descriptor isolation or imply that a memory-backed file is persisted.

The pinned nix 0.31.3 dependency also exposes `nix::unistd::pipe` and,
with the enabled `fs` feature, `pipe2`. Both create local interprocess
channels without invoking `std::net`, Unix-domain sockets, or `std::io`
stream operations; `pipe` descriptors do not have close-on-exec set by
default. Rust 1.97.1 compiled both captured function items against the
locked dependency configuration.

A red-first guard initially permitted an unreviewed pipe. The existing
standard-stream inventory now requires descriptor-channel authority review
for both constructors, including direct calls, captured function items,
renamed and nested imports, raw identifiers, and comment-separated paths.
Eight temporarily injected, cfg-disabled backend probes each failed the
owner check and were restored byte-for-byte. This is source-level review,
not proof of runtime pipe isolation or cross-process transfer prevention.

A third pinned nix 0.31.3 `fs` API can change process-wide filesystem
resolution: Linux `nix::unistd::pivot_root`. The locked Rust 1.97.1
compiler accepted its captured function item, unlike pathconf/fpathconf,
which require the disabled `feature` flag and were excluded from scope.

The process-global path mutation inventory did not recognize pivot_root;
a red-first test confirmed that gap. The zero-owner guard now rejects
its calls and function items, including aliased, nested, raw-identifier,
and comment-separated forms. Seven temporarily injected cfg-disabled
backend mutations were each rejected, then restored byte-for-byte.
This source check does not prove process isolation or replace runtime
confinement, and the existing browser-opener argv issue remains open.

The Rust 1.97.1 standard library also exposes
`std::thread::available_parallelism`, which reports host CPU availability
without a nix sysinfo call or environment read. A compiler-only fixture
confirmed that the function item is available. The ambient input source
inventory initially missed this host-status read in a red-first test.

The zero-owner privacy review now catches qualified and bare calls,
captured function items, renamed or grouped imports, raw identifiers,
module aliases, and comment-separated paths. The existing browser-opener
`thread::sleep` remains allowed; no new runtime observation is performed.
Nine temporary cfg-inactive `session-secret` source probes were each
rejected and restored byte-for-byte. This does not establish hardware
fingerprinting resistance or mitigate the known opener argv disclosure.

A complete real-Firefox continuation exercised all 42 browser integration
cases, including hostile content, HTTP security policy, malformed and stalled
session responses, clipboard copying, credential-fragment scrubbing, session
teardown, and keyboard layout behavior. All 42 passed without skips; this
closes the previously incomplete browser test run, not P8 acceptance itself.

A red-first browser privacy review found that the existing source checks
allowed an inert clipboard-read reference in an authored TypeScript helper.
The new browser host-input inventory permits the single explicit Copy prompt
clipboard-write acquisition in each main module while requiring review for
clipboard reads, geolocation, media devices, permissions, credentials, and
selected hardware or browser fingerprinting APIs. It scans all five authored
TypeScript modules and all five generated JavaScript counterparts.

Four deliberately inactive TypeScript injections (ordinary clipboard read,
computed clipboard read, geolocation, and optionally indexed CPU availability)
and one emitted-JavaScript media-device injection were each rejected by the
new owner check, then restored byte-for-byte. This is deliberately conservative
source-level evidence, not an AST or alias-flow proof, a browser permission
sandbox, packet-level isolation, or a fix for the known opener argv credential
exposure. The release task remains open pending its wider acceptance criteria.

The browser transport inventory previously covered four same-origin fetch
calls but did not review URL navigation, form submission or element-driven
requests that can bypass fetch entirely. A red-first probe inserted an inactive
`window.location.assign(dynamicTarget)` into an authored browser helper; the
existing transport and cross-context guards both passed it.

A second browser source inventory now pins the main module's two local-path
`history.replaceState` fragment scrub calls, one local-path
`location.replace` fallback, and one bfcache `location.reload`. New direct or
computed location changes, history navigation, form submission, synthetic
anchor clicks and dynamic image/audio constructors require review across
both the authored TypeScript and emitted JavaScript trees. Five inert
TypeScript navigation probes and one emitted-JavaScript probe were rejected,
then each mutated file was restored byte-for-byte.

The source inventory is not a DOM flow analysis or browser network sandbox;
dynamically aliased and
other unmodeled request paths remain outside the proof. The known browser
opener argv credential exposure is unchanged, and P8 remains open.

A follow-up red-first mutation proved the navigation inventory initially
missed `window['location'].assign(...)`. The review now includes literal
bracket access and `globalThis` for location and history navigation; direct,
computed-literal and globalThis probes pass the stricter regression. Arbitrary
computed property names and indirect aliases remain outside the static proof.

The existing browser privacy tests reviewed ten TypeScript/JavaScript
modules but did not inspect the static HTML/CSS shell for resource requests.
A red-first probe inserted an external `<img>` into the HTML and an `@import`
into the stylesheet; all 39 existing session-persistence tests still passed.
Both files were restored byte-for-byte after the probe.

A separate static-resource inventory now pins the favicon, stylesheet,
module script and two local skip links as the only declared HTML request or
navigation targets. It rejects new resource tags, URL-bearing attributes,
inline handlers, and meta redirects, along with CSS imports, URL/image
functions and font-face declarations. Two focused tests cover the tracked
sources and synthetic request variants.

Four temporary HTML/CSS mutations (external image, meta redirect, CSS
import, image-set) individually failed the
new guard and were restored exactly. This is conservative text-level source
review, not a DOM/CSS parser, runtime network trace, or substitute for CSP.
The opener-argv credential exposure and wider P8 acceptance remain open.

The four-direct-`fetch(...)` inventory overlooked first-class fetch function
references. An inactive `globalThis["fetch"]` in authored TypeScript and a
captured bare `fetch` in emitted JavaScript each passed all 41 prior
session-persistence tests, before both modules were byte-restored.

The browser transport review now counts every literal `fetch` identifier
outside standalone comments, in both authored and emitted modules, while
retaining exactly four direct calls in each main module. Seven synthetic
references, including property access, destructuring, call, and comment-
separated invocation, fail the new regression. The review does not resolve
computed names assembled at runtime or guarantee packet-level confinement.

The cross-context browser source guard previously checked literal
`window.open(` only. An inactive `globalThis["open"]` in TypeScript and a
captured bare `open` in emitted JavaScript each passed all 42 prior
session-persistence tests, then the source bytes were restored exactly.

The zero-owner popup review now catches direct and captured global `open`
identifiers in every authored and emitted module, including bracket access,
optional chaining and destructuring. Eight syntactic probes and four separate
inactive source mutations confirm this guard detects new popup authority.
This is static review rather than a user-gesture or live-browser popup test;
computed runtime names are not exhaustively covered.

A separate browser file-authority review found that first-class
`globalThis["showOpenFilePicker"]` in authored TypeScript and
`navigator["storage"].getDirectory()` in emitted JavaScript passed all 43
previous session-persistence tests. The original modules were restored.

The new browser source guard reviews named File System Access pickers, legacy
filesystem methods, drag-and-drop filesystem handles, and indexed navigator
storage references across both TypeScript and emitted JavaScript. Twelve
syntactic variants and four temporary inactive source mutations now fail
before exact restoration. A picker still requires user participation, while
